        "rationale": "Scabies mites form tiny raised crooked or serpiginous burrows that may be grayish-white or skin colored. The web spaces between fingers and the folds of the wrist are characteristic sites, so c0 supplies both the most specific morphology and distribution. Papules and itching can occur with scabies but are not as specific as an observed burrow. A similar-looking line in a noncharacteristic dry-shin setting lacks the characteristic site combination, and transient changing wheals do not match the described burrow pattern. The 10-year-old's age keeps the item within the usual older-child distribution; infants and very young children can have a broader rash distribution involving the head, face, neck, palms and soles. The selected finding supports possible scabies assessment but does not independently prove infestation, select a diagnostic procedure, or authorize treatment.",
        "difficultyReason": "The learner directly recognizes the most characteristic observed morphology-and-distribution combination: thin raised crooked skin-colored lines in finger webs and wrist folds. The stem supplies one stable assessment moment and asks only which finding most specifically supports possible scabies. No transmission inference, diagnostic confirmation, treatment, contact plan, post-treatment response, severity comparison, competing trajectory, or multiple-step decision is required. This is foundational Level 1 recognition, is explicitly non-hard, and can never fill hard-80 inventory.",
