      "objective": "Prioritize breathing and withhold unsafe oral intake during deterioration",
      "stem": "A newborn born at 30 weeks is now 35 weeks postmenstrual age and has been taking partial oral feedings. Over the past hour, oxygen support has increased, retractions have become more pronounced, and the newborn tires with routine care. An oral feeding is scheduled now. Which nursing plan should be prioritized?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "c0",
          "text": "Withhold the oral attempt, maintain prescribed respiratory support and monitoring, and notify the neonatal team for reassessment."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1",
          "text": "Replace the oral attempt with the full planned gavage volume now while leaving the respiratory plan unchanged."
        },
        {
          "id": "c2",
          "text": "Complete a full oral-readiness score and observed sucking trial before reporting the increased retractions to the neonatal team."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3",
          "text": "Cluster care and allow a rest period, then retry the scheduled oral feeding if the newborn appears less tired on the same support."
        }
      ],
      "hard80Eligible": null,
      "answerReview": {
        "clue": "The newborn's support need and work of breathing are worsening before the feeding begins; apparent rest alone does not show that the trend has resolved.",
        "rationale": "Prioritize breathing and withhold oral intake during respiratory deterioration. Maintain ordered support and monitoring and obtain team reassessment before selecting an oral or alternate nutrition route. The cues indicate compromise and reduced feeding safety; they do not by themselves prove respiratory failure or prescribe a new device, oxygen concentration, or gavage volume.",
        "difficultyReason": "The learner applies a one-hour respiratory trend and feeding demand to distinguish immediate stabilization from plausible alternate-route, readiness-assessment, and rest-first plans. The aligned trend supports level 2.",
        "limitations": [
          "The case indicates respiratory deterioration or compromise, not confirmed respiratory failure.",
          "No oxygen target, device change, gavage volume, feeding-readiness tool, or time-to-retry rule is specified.",
          "AHA is supplemental respiratory guidance; later NICU feeding and support decisions remain course-, ATI-, order-, and condition-dependent.",
          "The case supports respiratory deterioration/compromise, not confirmed respiratory failure.",
          "No oxygen target, device change, gavage volume, readiness tool, alternate route, or time-to-retry rule is claimed."
        ],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "c0",
            "correct": true,
            "reason": "Increasing support needs, work of breathing, and fatigue indicate deterioration and make an oral feeding attempt unsafe until respiratory stability and feeding readiness are reassessed."
          },
          {
            "id": "c1",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "A gavage route may later be appropriate, but completing nutrition before reassessing the respiratory deterioration assumes feeding tolerance and leaves the changing support need unaddressed."
          },
          {
            "id": "c2",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "Readiness assessment is useful in stable infants, but an oral trial should not delay escalation when breathing and support needs are already worsening."
          },
          {
            "id": "c3",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "Energy conservation is supportive but does not establish respiratory stability or feeding readiness after a trend of increasing support and retractions."
          }
        ]
      },
