      "objective": "Escalate after glucose treatment failure with reduced responsiveness",
      "stem": "A term newborn is 3 hours old and is being treated under the unit hypoglycemia pathway. After a feeding and prescribed dextrose gel, glucose remains below the pathway's treatment target. The newborn is now difficult to arouse and cannot suck safely. Which action has priority?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "c0",
          "text": "Notify the neonatal team immediately and prepare to administer prescribed IV glucose with continuous clinical monitoring."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1",
          "text": "Attempt another feeding before notifying the team, using stimulation to maintain sucking."
        },
        {
          "id": "c2",
          "text": "Give another gel dose and defer the IV plan until the response to gel is known."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3",
          "text": "Obtain a laboratory confirmation and wait for that result before escalating treatment."
        }
      ],
      "hard80Eligible": null,
      "answerReview": {
        "clue": "The newborn has both failed initial therapy and lost the ability to use the oral route safely.",
        "rationale": "Escalate immediately when glucose remains below the local treatment target and the newborn develops reduced responsiveness with unsafe sucking. The neonatal plan should move toward prescribed IV glucose while airway, breathing, circulation, and glucose response are monitored. Additional gel can be a directed bridge during IV access in some protocols, but it must not become a reason to delay definitive rescue.",
        "difficultyReason": "The learner must integrate treatment failure, neurologic change, and loss of a safe oral route to choose the next rescue pathway over believable delay strategies, supporting level 2.",
        "limitations": [
          "No glucose number, IV concentration, bolus or infusion dose, repeat-testing interval, or gel schedule is introduced.",
          "The case establishes a local treatment-target failure and symptoms, not a universal jurisdictional threshold.",
          "Gel is not characterized as categorically inappropriate; its possible temporizing role remains protocol- and team-directed.",
          "The evidence permits gel as a directed bridge while IV access is established; it does not permit gel to delay IV rescue or turn the local pathway target into a universal threshold."
        ],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "c0",
            "correct": true,
            "reason": "Persistent low glucose with reduced responsiveness and an unsafe oral route requires urgent reassessment and parenteral rescue under the neonatal plan."
          },
          {
            "id": "c1",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "The newborn cannot suck safely and has neurologic deterioration after initial treatment, so another oral attempt would delay the needed escalation."
          },
          {
            "id": "c2",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "Gel may be used as a temporary bridge when directed while IV access is being established, but it should not postpone parenteral rescue in a symptomatic newborn who cannot feed safely."
          },
          {
            "id": "c3",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "Confirmatory testing may be obtained within the pathway, but treatment of a symptomatic newborn should not be held while awaiting the result."
          }
        ]
      },
