      "objective": "Coordinate ordered specimens and timely empiric therapy",
      "stem": "A newborn with suspected infection has temperature instability, tachypnea, and delayed capillary refill. The neonatal clinician orders a blood culture, baseline laboratory tests, and empiric IV antibiotics while stabilization continues. Which nursing actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "c0",
          "text": "Obtain the ordered blood culture before the first antibiotic dose when this can occur without unsafe treatment delay."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1",
          "text": "Administer the empiric IV antibiotics as ordered without waiting for culture or other laboratory results."
        },
        {
          "id": "c2",
          "text": "Continue reassessing respiratory effort, oxygenation, perfusion, glucose, and urine output during specimen collection and treatment."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3",
          "text": "After collecting the blood culture, hold the ready first antibiotic until the next routine collection round obtains the remaining ordered baseline specimens."
        },
        {
          "id": "c4",
          "text": "Use normalization of temperature after warming to postpone the first dose until the next full assessment clarifies whether infection remains likely."
        }
      ],
      "hard80Eligible": false,
      "answerReview": {
        "clue": "Diagnostic yield matters, but specimens, stabilization, and empiric treatment must be coordinated so no single step creates an unsafe delay.",
        "rationale": "Obtain the ordered blood culture before the first antibiotic dose when feasible, then give empiric therapy promptly without awaiting results or holding the ready first dose for a later routine collection round. Continue system-focused reassessment during treatment. The specimen list depends on the clinical setting and orders, and one response to warming does not supersede the treatment plan.",
        "difficultyReason": "The learner applies timing and role constraints across specimens, empiric therapy, and systemic monitoring while evaluating five independent options. The order and clinical cues are explicit, so this remains level 2.",
        "limitations": [
          "No antibiotic name, dose, interval, duration, laboratory panel, lumbar-puncture indication, or culture-site bundle is generalized.",
          "CPS guidance is bounded to symptomatic term early-onset sepsis context, not every neonatal infection/workup.",
          "No antibiotic name, dose, interval, duration, laboratory panel, lumbar-puncture indication, or culture-site bundle is claimed."
        ],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "c0",
            "correct": true,
            "reason": "A pretreatment blood culture improves diagnostic yield, but the qualification prevents specimen collection from becoming a barrier to urgent care."
          },
          {
            "id": "c1",
            "correct": true,
            "reason": "Empiric treatment addresses possible infection before organism identification; final results guide later team decisions rather than the timing of the initial ordered dose."
          },
          {
            "id": "c2",
            "correct": true,
            "reason": "Newborn infection can affect respiratory, circulatory, metabolic, neurologic, and renal status, so treatment coordination includes continued physiologic surveillance."
          },
          {
            "id": "c3",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "Once the ordered blood culture is collected and the first antibiotic is ready, routine scheduling of other baseline specimens should not postpone empiric therapy for a symptomatic newborn."
          },
          {
            "id": "c4",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "One improving sign does not negate tachypnea, delayed refill, and the clinician's empiric-treatment order. Reassessment continues while timely treatment proceeds."
          }
        ]
      },
