      "objective": "Differentiate a stable caput from cephalohematoma and evolving subgaleal hemorrhage",
      "stem": "One hour after a vaginal birth, a newborn has soft, edematous scalp swelling that was present at birth and extends across suture lines. During serial assessments, the swelling and head circumference remain unchanged. Color, heart rate, and perfusion are within the nursery's expected ranges. Which plan should the nurse follow?",
      "options": [
        {
          "id": "c0",
          "text": "Document a cephalohematoma, continue serial assessment, and add bilirubin surveillance because the newborn remains clinically stable."
        },
        {
          "id": "c1",
          "text": "Document a finding consistent with caput, continue routine serial assessment, and explain that it usually resolves without treatment."
        },
        {
          "id": "c2",
          "text": "Notify the neonatal team for urgent subgaleal evaluation and begin intensified head-circumference and perfusion monitoring."
        },
        {
          "id": "c3",
          "text": "Document caput and provide reassurance, then discontinue serial scalp and head-circumference checks after the initial stable assessment."
        }
      ],
      "hard80Eligible": false,
      "answerReview": {
        "clue": "Use both the suture-line pattern and the absence of progression or systemic deterioration.",
        "rationale": "A soft scalp edema that crosses suture lines, is present at birth, and remains stable without perfusion changes is consistent with caput succedaneum. Continue routine serial newborn assessment and give reassuring teaching because caput usually resolves without treatment. A cephalohematoma is confined by suture lines and adds bilirubin-surveillance concerns. Diffuse swelling that enlarges or accompanies pallor, tachycardia, delayed refill, or increasing head circumference requires urgent evaluation for subgaleal hemorrhage. Crossing a suture line alone does not make an emergency, and initial stability supports routine—not discontinued—serial assessment.",
        "difficultyReason": "Estimated level 2 because the learner must integrate suture behavior with serial stability and systemic findings, then distinguish routine monitoring from plausible cephalohematoma, subgaleal, and premature-monitoring-closure plans.",
        "limitations": [
          "The case supports a stable caput pattern; it does not claim that every swelling crossing sutures is benign.",
          "No imaging rule, aspiration procedure, bilirubin threshold, surveillance interval, or subgaleal resuscitation protocol is introduced.",
          "A change in swelling, head circumference, color, heart rate, or perfusion would require reassessment rather than continued reassurance."
        ],
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "c0",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "Clinical stability does not distinguish caput from cephalohematoma. The swelling crosses sutures, which supports caput rather than the suture-confined cephalohematoma pattern."
          },
          {
            "id": "c1",
            "correct": true,
            "reason": "Caput is soft scalp edema that can cross sutures. Stability of the swelling, head circumference, and perfusion supports routine observation and reassuring teaching."
          },
          {
            "id": "c2",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "Cross-suture swelling can occur with subgaleal hemorrhage, but urgent escalation is supported by progression or systemic blood-loss findings. This collection and the newborn remain stable."
          },
          {
            "id": "c3",
            "correct": false,
            "reason": "The current findings support caput and reassurance, but routine serial assessment should continue so a change in swelling, circumference, or perfusion is recognized."
          }
        ]
      },
